  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Is photography a good career in India? It can be, but only for people who pick a genre with paying clients and run it like a small business.</strong>
    The camera is the cheap part. The hard parts are finding clients, setting a price you can live on, and keeping up with tools that now make some kinds of pictures for free. A strong <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong>
    (camera craft, editing, video, AI tools and basic selling) is what turns photography into <strong>high income opportunities</strong> and a path toward <strong>earlier financial freedom</strong>. A pile of course certificates does not.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Photography is a good career for people who treat it as a business, and a weak one if you expect a steady salary for taking good pictures. The genre decides most of the outcome.</li>
      <li>Wedding and event work, photo plus video packages, and repeat brand work carry the most real clients. Plain catalogue shots and stock are the most exposed to AI.</li>
      <li>No degree or licence is required. Clients look at your portfolio and your delivered jobs. Keep a course to about 10% of your education budget, and rent gear before you buy it.</li>
      <li><strong>The decisions that unlock high income opportunities here are genre, price, proof of work and how clients find and trust you. They matter more than the camera brand.</strong></li>
      <li>Next step: deliver one small paid job from start to finish before you buy gear or pay for a course.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

