  <h2 id="verdict">Is photography a good career? The short answer</h2>

  <p>
    Photography is not one career. It is at least eight, and they pay, scale and face AI in very different ways. A wedding photographer, a product photographer and a newsroom photographer have almost nothing in common except the camera.
  </p>

  <p>
    So the useful question is not "is photography good". It is "which route, at what price, for which paying client, and what happens when a tool can make the same picture for free".
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Anyone with a phone can take a decent picture, so taking pictures is not the skill that pays. What pays is turning up reliably, delivering on time, making clients feel looked after, and having proof that other people trust. Photography is a craft first and a sales job second, and most people learn the craft and skip the sales.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    A simple stack works in every genre: one core skill (camera craft in your genre), one multiplier skill (video, fast editing, AI tools, or selling), and visible proof (delivered jobs and a tight portfolio). </p>

  <p>
    Test any route with four questions. Is there a human edge that tools do not copy? Is there an urgent problem for the client, such as sales or a once-only event? Is there room to raise prices? Does it take enough practice to filter out casual competition?
  </p>

  <h2 id="genre-map">Photography genres, ranked by how far they can scale</h2>

  <p>
    The list leads with the routes that have the most room to grow income, and ends with the ones where very few people succeed. Crowded routes stay in the list so you can see the risk beside them. Each has a scalability label from our own rating.
  </p>

