        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take: two-stage competition</p>
    <p>
      Fashion, portrait, wildlife and newsroom work have two hard filters. The first is getting your first paid jobs. The second is climbing from those jobs to a name that clients and editors seek out. Many people clear the first and stall at the second. Plan a second genre that pays while you try for the harder one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="pay">What photographers are paid, and where each number comes from</h2>

  <p>
    There is no trustworthy survey of photographer pay in India. What exists is a mix of job listings, course-seller pages and studio price lists. The table names every source so you can judge it yourself.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>What it measures</th>
          <th>Range shown</th>
          <th>Source</th>
          <th>How far to trust it</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {payRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="What it measures"><strong>{item.what}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Range shown">{item.range}</td>
            <td data-label="Source">{item.source}</td>
            <td data-label="How far to trust it">{item.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    None of these are median claims. A yearly figure of ₹8 to 15 lakh is a strong outcome for an experienced photographer with steady clients, not what a first-year photographer should plan on.
  </p>

  <p class="note-line">
    Pay figures here are indicative ranges and averages from public profiles, not exact or guaranteed amounts. Pay differs by city, employer, skill and year, and markets change. Check current job listings (for example on LinkedIn, Naukri or Indeed) and ask people working in the role before you decide.
  </p>

  <p>
    One more number helps with perspective. A trade body's wedding-season breakdown, reported by Tribune India in November 2024, listed photography and videography at about 2 per cent of wedding spending in the services segment. The same report estimated about 48 lakh weddings that season. </p>

  <p>
    The estimate comes from a traders' body, not an audit, but it shows that your fee is a small slice of a large family budget, so the family's trust in you matters more than shaving your price.
  </p>

  <h2 id="price-floor">How to find your price floor</h2>

  <p>
    Charge for the job, not the clock. But first know the number below which a job loses you money. Here is the method.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
    {priceSteps.map((item) => (
      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div class="checkpoint__body">
          <div class="checkpoint__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    When a client says "let me think about it", ask what is holding them back before you cut the price. Raise your rates as your dates fill up. Staying at a beginner price long after you have outgrown it is a more common mistake than charging too much.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      <strong>Made-up numbers to show the method, not market rates:</strong> if you need ₹30,000 a month (₹3.6 lakh a year), your business costs are ₹1.2 lakh a year, and you can deliver 40 jobs, your floor is (₹3.6 lakh plus ₹1.2 lakh) divided by 40, which is ₹12,000 per job before tax. Use your own inputs. If you can only deliver 20 jobs, the same floor doubles.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="season">Wedding season, lean months and cash flow</h2>

  <p>
    Wedding income follows the calendar of auspicious dates, not your effort. A trade body listed 18 auspicious dates between 12 November and 16 December in the 2024-25 season, then a pause of about a month before the season resumed in mid-January through March. Dates change every year with the panchang, so check the current one.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What the season does to you</div>
      <ul>
        <li>A few weeks of back-to-back days, then quiet weeks with no income.</li>
        <li>Editing piles up after the rush, so delivery slips just when clients start asking for photos.</li>
        <li>Everyone competes for the same dates, which is why a calendar of booked dates and deposits matters.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What to do about it</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Treat peak-season earnings as money for twelve months, not as a monthly pay.</li>
        <li>Fill lean months with product, portrait, brand or real-estate work, and with editing for other studios.</li>
        <li>Take a deposit to hold each date, and stop taking dates you cannot edit on time.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="gear">Gear: rent first, buy when the jobs pay for it</h2>

  <p>
    Gear is where new photographers lose money. The rule is simple: a purchase must be paid for by jobs you have already booked, not jobs you hope for.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Rental break-even</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">Price of gear divided by day rent</div>
      <p>That is the number of paid days the gear needs. If you will not reach it within a year, keep renting. Listings we saw show premium Sony lenses around ₹1,200 to ₹2,000 a day in Delhi and a Canon 5D Mark IV around ₹1,500 a day in Navi Mumbai. Confirm current rates yourself.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Buy in this order</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">What limits your work first</div>
      <p>Editing laptop and storage backups come first, because they decide delivery time. Then a second memory card or dual-slot body so one failed card does not ruin a wedding. Then lighting. The camera body is often the last real limit.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Money rule</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">No loan for gear before paid jobs</div>
      <p>A monthly instalment on a camera turns a slow start into debt. Used gear and rentals let you earn first, then upgrade with your own money.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="learning">Course or portfolio: how to learn this</h2>

  <p>
    No degree or licence is needed to work as a photographer. The US labour-data office lists a high school diploma as the usual entry level, with classes or a certificate as optional help. </p>

  <p>
    In India, diplomas are widely sold. One listing site shows fees from about ₹20,000 to ₹2 lakh for a one-year diploma, and some private institutes list more. We found no reliable placement data for photography courses.
  </p>

  <p>
    Information is free: YouTube lessons, camera makers' guides and free editing tutorials teach the basics. The paid value must be something you cannot get free: feedback on real work, supervised shoots, equipment access or a network.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Pay for a course only if it gives you</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Teachers who currently take paid jobs and can show recent client work.</li>
        <li>Practice on real briefs, not only on practice subjects.</li>
        <li>Lighting, editing and a business module (pricing, contracts, client handling).</li>
        <li>Written feedback on your work, and refund terms in writing.</li>
        <li>Past students' work and phone numbers of three graduates you can call.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Walk away if</div>
      <ul>
        <li>The pitch is a certificate and a guarantee of work.</li>
        <li>Fees go above roughly 20% of your education budget without clear proof of outcomes.</li>
        <li>The syllabus is camera buttons and nothing about clients.</li>
        <li>You are asked to take a loan.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    A cheaper and often stronger route is to assist a working photographer for a stretch. You learn real lighting, client handling and delivery, and you meet the people who send work later. For a degree-level route, design and fine-arts institutes run their own entrance tests, so read each institute's official admission page.
  </p>

  <p>
    Use the 10% starting rule: if your total education and starting budget is ₹3 lakh, about ₹30,000 goes to a course, and the rest pays for rentals, editing tools, storage, travel and a few lean months. Above roughly 20%, ask for course-specific proof. Treat both as planning rules, not facts.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who-pays">Who pays you, and how they find and trust you</h2>

  <p>
    Every genre has a payer, and the payer needs a reason to pick you. Learn the basics of selling: say who you help and with what, offer a simple package, show proof, follow up, ask for referrals, and have one honest talk about price. Do not promise income to yourself or to clients.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Route</th>
          <th>Who pays</th>
          <th>How they trust you</th>
          <th>First sales skill</th>
          <th>One number to watch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {whoPaysRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Route"><strong>{item.route}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Who pays">{item.pays}</td>
            <td data-label="How they trust you">{item.trust}</td>
            <td data-label="First sales skill">{item.skill}</td>
            <td data-label="One number to watch">{item.number}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The usual order is your own network first, then referrals from it, then one repeatable channel such as Instagram or a listing site. </p>

  <p>
    A good first message to a planner or brand has three parts: one specific thing you liked about their work, a link to one relevant set of yours, and one small question. Show your work from the first job on one main platform, built deeply before you add others, and keep a short list of clients and planners you can message directly. Communication in clear English helps with brand clients and outstation work. Keep a contact list you own, so one app change does not cut off your enquiries.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rules">Rights, GST and drone rules every photographer should know</h2>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Copyright</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">The client may own the photos by default</div>
      <p>Under Section 17 of the Copyright Act 1957, a photograph made for payment at someone's request belongs to that person unless an agreement says otherwise. Work done as an employee generally belongs to the employer. If you want to keep rights, or reuse photos in your portfolio, write it into the contract before the shoot.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">GST</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">Check the turnover limit early</div>
      <p>Services providers generally need GST registration once yearly turnover crosses ₹20 lakh, or ₹10 lakh in special-category states, per a ClearTax summary. Foreign software and platform fees can also bring GST duties. Confirm limits on the official GST portal and ask a chartered accountant. Udyam registration is free and online once work is regular.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Drones</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">A licence may be needed to fly for money</div>
      <p>Summaries of the Drone Rules 2021 say most pilots need a remote pilot licence from an approved training school, with age 18 to 65 and class 10 passed, and that nano drones and non-commercial micro flights are treated more lightly (summaries differ, so check). Airspace is green, yellow or red. Check the DGCA site and the Digital Sky platform for current rules, because a 2025 draft bill could change them.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ai">What AI changes in each genre</h2>

  <p>
    AI is not one threat. It hits plain, repeatable pictures first and real-world presence last. Rows marked "our judgement" are our own reading, because we found no Indian data.
  </p>

