  <h3>A staged way to use AI</h3>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Start now</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Try AI culling and batch editing on your own past shoots and compare the time saved. Do not trust the makers' timings.</li>
        <li>Learn AI retouching and cleanup so you deliver faster.</li>
        <li>Add a line to your contract on how AI tools are used and how client photos are stored.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Build toward</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Offer real product shots plus extra variations made from them, with the AI work clearly named.</li>
        <li>Move into art direction: a brand's look, a shot list and a consistent style that tools cannot decide for a client.</li>
        <li>Check each tool's commercial-use terms and get written consent when real people appear in AI work.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="layers">Business layers that make photography scale</h2>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>The scalable-ownership path in photography is a small studio, not a solo photographer who does every shoot and every edit.</strong>
      It scales because you set the prices, hire second shooters and editors, sell packages with video, and keep repeat and referral clients. Your income then depends on your system and your name, not only your own hours.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {layerCards.map((item) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="feature-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    Work style matters too. Shoots are local, but editing, retouching and album design can be done from anywhere, so a photographer in a smaller city can take edit work from studios in bigger cities while the local client base grows. </p>

  <p>
    Know your numbers before you hire anyone. Compare the cost of winning a client with what that client is worth over time, and look at what is left after tax, travel and helpers. Keep business and personal money separate, and pay yourself a set amount.
  </p>

  <h2 id="burnout">Burnout and irregular income</h2>

  <p>
    The strain is real and mostly hidden in the pictures online. Peak season means weekend after weekend, long days on your feet and family functions missed. The edit then follows at night. </p>

  <p>
    One tool maker puts the editing time for a single wedding at 8 to 14 hours, which is a vendor claim, but it matches how photographers describe the backlog.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Income arrives in bursts. Build a cash buffer in the busy months to cover the quiet ones.</li>
    <li>Set a limit on dates per season so delivery does not slip.</li>
    <li>Say no to work outside your scope or below your floor price.</li>
    <li>Hire or outsource editing before your health forces you to.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="skip">Who should skip photography as a career</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
